1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,404
What stops one admin from switching on a fraudulent shop?

2
00:00:03,404 --> 00:00:08,142
Right now one person can, so one mistake or one bribe is enough.

3
00:00:08,142 --> 00:00:15,000
So activation becomes a request: one admin, the maker, asks, and nothing changes yet.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,738
The single-admin activate call is gone, and three new calls take its place.

5
00:00:19,738 --> 00:00:25,535
A different admin, the checker, approves it, and only then does the merchant become active.

6
00:00:25,535 --> 00:00:32,178
A request is decided once: it must still be pending, not expired, and the checker can't be the maker.

7
00:00:32,178 --> 00:00:36,202
Self-approval answers four oh nine, with its own error code.

8
00:00:36,202 --> 00:00:40,654
The checker's name goes into the status history, with the maker in the reason.

9
00:00:40,654 --> 00:00:44,845
Requests expire after a configured time, twenty-four hours here.

10
00:00:44,845 --> 00:00:51,511
A scheduled sweep marks overdue requests as expired, and ShedLock makes sure only one instance runs it.

11
00:00:51,511 --> 00:00:58,488
Only one pending request per merchant can exist, and a partial unique index in the database enforces it.

12
00:00:58,488 --> 00:01:04,083
Integration tests on a real Postgres prove the approval, the index, and the sweep.

13
00:01:04,083 --> 00:01:12,285
Under load, almost three thousand merchants were activated by two admins in thirty seconds, and every self-approval was refused.

14
00:01:12,285 --> 00:01:17,142
Next, real logins, so an admin's name comes from a token instead of a header.

